The general solution is $u(t) = e^{\alpha t} (C_1 \cos(\beta t) + C_2 \sin(\beta t))$.
Substitute initial conditions $u(0)=2$ and $u'(0)=1$:
$u(0) = e^0 (C_1 \cdot 1 + C_2 \cdot 0) = C_1 = 2$.
$u'(t) = \alpha e^{\alpha t}(C_1 \cos \beta t + C_2 \sin \beta t) + e^{\alpha t}(-C_1 \beta \sin \beta t + C_2 \beta \cos \beta t)$.
